CH = dict(num=1, title="Exploration: Entering the World of Secondary Science", tag="Introduction", theme="Models, language of science, laws and theories, prediction, estimation",
intro="This opening chapter is not about one topic. It explains **how** secondary science works: scientists simplify nature with models, speak a precise language of symbols and units, build laws and theories from evidence, make testable predictions, and reason with estimates. The two symbols on every page — a **magnifying glass** (careful observation) and a **compass** (direction and purpose) — sum up the message.",
big_questions=["Why does science use models instead of studying everything in full detail?", "Why must scientific language and units be precise?", "What is the difference between a law, a theory and a principle?", "How do predictions and estimates help us test ideas?"],
blocks=[
("h2", "1.1 From Wonder to Deeper Exploration"),
("ul", ["In the middle stage, science meant being curious, observing, questioning and finding how things work; ideas of the living and non-living world were linked.",
 "In the secondary stage the emphasis shifts to **deep exploration**: how observations lead to measurements, how patterns are written as symbols and equations, how **models** are built, and how ideas are tested, revised and sometimes discarded.",
 "Science is therefore not only *what* we know but *how* we know it."]),
("table", ["Symbol on the page numbers", "What it stands for"], [["Magnifying glass", "Careful observation — noticing patterns and details that are easy to miss"], ["Compass", "Direction — choosing suitable models, asking the right questions and knowing the limits of where an idea applies"]]),
("note", "REMEMBER", "Exploration in science is not wandering aimlessly; it is trying to make sense of the world **with care and purpose**."),

("h2", "1.2 Models: Simplifying a Complex World"),
("p", "The natural world is too complex to study in every detail, so science uses **models** — simplified ways of looking at a real system that keep only what matters for a given question. Leaving out details is done **on purpose**; it is not a mistake."),
("table", ["Branch", "How a model simplifies"], [["Physics", "A moving car is treated as a single point"], ["Chemistry", "Atoms and molecules are drawn as spheres, with sticks for bonds"], ["Biology", "A cell is shown as a diagram highlighting only the key parts"], ["Earth science", "The Earth is treated as a smooth sphere made of distinct layers"]]),
("ul", ["Building a model involves **assumptions** — deliberately ignoring certain details. Example: when studying a falling object we may neglect **air resistance** to see the basic effect of gravity.",
 "In biology, to understand how the heart pumps blood, individual cells are ignored and the heart is seen as a working system.",
 "As more detail is added, the model becomes more complex but also more accurate."]),
("h3", "Example 1.1 — Modelling a cricket six"),
("p", "Key question: *will the ball cross the boundary without hitting the ground first?*"),
("table", ["Keep (important)", "Small effect (can ignore in a simple model)", "No effect (ignore)"], [["Mass of the ball; speed and direction of the hit", "Air resistance; spin; stitching of the seam", "Brand of the bat; colour of the ball; grass on the field"]]),
("h3", "Activity 1.1 — Time to cycle home from school"),
("p", "Keep: distance, average speed, stops at traffic lights. Ignore: colour of the cycle, the weather on one day, small speed variations. Ignoring details is useful because it keeps the model simple enough to give an answer."),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "**Meghnad Saha** (shown on an Indian postage stamp) studied starlight. He did not model every atom or reaction; he treated star matter as a **hot gas** and focused on **temperature, pressure and how atoms form ions**. This simplification explained why the **colour of a star is linked to its temperature**."),

("h2", "1.3 The Language of Science: Symbols, Units and Mathematics"),
("ul", ["Everyday words such as **force**, **work** or **reaction** have **specific meanings** in science, because scientific ideas must be communicated clearly and without ambiguity.",
 "Quantities are written as symbols (mass **m**, velocity **v**, force **F**, current **I**), each with a defined **unit**, so scientists across the world can describe observations, compare results and build ideas together.",
 "Mathematics is a **language** for expressing relationships between quantities and testing them. An equation is a compact statement of how things are related, not just a calculating tool.",
 "Good approach: first **understand the situation**, then identify the **relevant quantities**, then use a relation to reason — do not simply memorise equations."]),
("ul", ["Examples of use: distance, time and velocity predict where a moving object will be later; mathematical expressions describe rates of chemical reactions, growth of populations and energy changes in a system."]),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "Scientific symbols come from history and **international agreement**, not always from convenience. The speed of light is written **c** from the Latin *celeritas* (speed). It is a physical constant defined to be exactly **299 792 458 m/s**."),
("h3", "Why standard (SI) units matter"),
("ul", ["Measurements rest on agreed **international standards**, not local objects or opinions, so a kilogram of rice means the same everywhere; this also gives fairness in trade.",
 "**Airplane fuel incident:** a flight needed 22 300 kg of fuel, but the crew used the fuel density in **pounds per litre** instead of **kilograms per litre**. The aircraft was about **15 000 litres short**, glided to an emergency landing and was damaged, though nobody was hurt.",
 "Lesson: use **SI units** consistently to avoid conversions and errors."]),

("h2", "1.4 Laws, Theories and Principles"),
("table", ["Term", "Meaning", "Example from the chapter"], [
 ["**Law**", "Describes a regular **pattern** seen in nature, in words or by a mathematical relation", "Newton's laws of motion explain the jerk when a bus stops suddenly"],
 ["**Theory**", "Goes further and **explains why** the pattern occurs, based on evidence gathered over time", "Atomic theory explains how molecules are formed"],
 ["**Principle**", "A broad idea that helps us make sense of a situation", "Conservation of energy applied while climbing stairs"]]),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "In science a **theory is not a guess**. It is an explanation based on careful testing and critical examination. Theories remain open to improvement and may change when new evidence appears — this is what makes science reliable."),

("h2", "1.5 Prediction and Testing Ideas"),
("ul", ["When laws, theories and models are well established they let us **predict** what will happen under new conditions, sometimes even when an experiment cannot be done.",
 "Examples: how far a kicked football travels (motion); how much carbon dioxide is produced or how soft baked bread will be (chemical reactions); how breathing changes while running (biology).",
 "A scientific prediction is a **reasoned expectation based on evidence**, not a guess. If it matches observation, confidence in the idea grows; if it does not, scientists re-examine assumptions, models or measurements."]),
("h3", "Example 1.2 — Testing 'It will rain because the clouds look dark'"),
("p", "Good scientific questions ask for **measurable data and past patterns**, not yes/no answers: What was the sky like when it last rained? Was humidity above 80 per cent last time? What is today's wind speed and direction? Is the temperature dropping as before the last rains?"),
("ul", ["**Why forecasts go wrong:** weather depends on temperature, pressure, humidity and wind; very tiny differences can grow with time, so forecasts are reliable for hours or a few days but less certain further ahead.",
 "Even the best theories have limits. Failure of a prediction is not a weakness — it is science's **greatest strength**, because ideas are rejected on **evidence**, never on opinion or belief. No theory is final or beyond question.",
 "**Viral claim check:** 'food becomes harmful during an eclipse'. An eclipse is only a play of shadows; no physical, chemical or biological mechanism makes food harmful, so the claim fails when tested with simple scientific questions."]),

("h2", "1.6 Estimation and Approximate Reasoning"),
("ul", ["Understand the situation → identify the quantities that matter → make a **rough estimate** to see whether an answer is reasonable or impossible.",
 "Estimating builds intuition, helps detect errors and gives confidence. Science values careful reasoning perhaps even more than precise calculation."]),
("h3", "Example 1.3 — Litres of air breathed in a day"),
("steps", [("Step 1 — Breaths per day", "About 12–15 breaths per minute at rest; a day has 60 × 24 = 1440 minutes, so about 18 000–22 000, i.e. roughly **20 000 breaths**."),
 ("Step 2 — Volume of one breath", "About 4–5 breaths fill a party balloon of about 2 litres, so one breath is about **0.5 litre**."),
 ("Step 3 — Combine", "20 000 × 0.5 ≈ **10 000 litres per day**."),
 ("Step 4 — Check", "Another route: about 3 balloons a minute × 2 litres × 1440 minutes ≈ 8640 litres, reasonably close to 10 000 litres, so the estimate is sensible.")]),
("ul", ["**Rice estimate (Ready to Go Beyond):** an adult needs about 2000–2500 kcal a day; find the calories in 100 g of cooked rice and scale up for four people for a month. The answer should be sensible — 100 g is far too little, a few tonnes far too much.",
 "Approximate answers suit situations like estimating travel time; exact values are needed in situations like medicine doses or fuel loading."]),

("h2", "1.7 Branches of Science and Their Connections"),
("ul", ["After Grade 10, science divides into **physics, chemistry, biology and earth science**; the 'Next Level Up' boxes in the textbook hint at what each may offer.",
 "These divisions are **human-made** to organise knowledge. Nature has no such boundaries, and real problems — climate change, medicines, sustainable technology — need ideas from several disciplines plus mathematics, technology, arts and social sciences.",
 "**Mask example:** physics (particle motion, electrostatic attraction), chemistry (polymer fibres), biology (size and behaviour of viruses) and mathematics (airflow and filtration efficiency) all explain how a mask works."]),
("h2", "1.8 Science as a Human Activity"),
("ul", ["Science is not just facts, equations and experiments; it is shaped by **curiosity, creativity, collaboration and careful questioning**.",
 "It grows as people ask questions, test ideas, share results and learn from mistakes, through the work of many people across cultures and generations.",
 "Scientific thinking helps you understand technology, evaluate information critically and make sense of the world, even if you do not study science after Grade 10."]),
("h2", "1.9 Quick Revision Questions"),
("qa", [
 ("What is a scientific model? Why are details ignored?", "A simplified representation of a real system that keeps only what matters for a question. Details are ignored on purpose so the problem stays simple enough to answer."),
 ("Give one example of a model from each of physics, chemistry and biology.", "Physics: car as a point. Chemistry: atoms as spheres with bonds. Biology: labelled cell diagram."),
 ("Why did Meghnad Saha treat star matter as a hot gas?", "To focus on temperature, pressure and ion formation; this explained why a star's colour depends on its temperature."),
 ("Why does science use SI units?", "They are international standards, so results can be compared and mistakes avoided; the airplane fuel mix-up shows the risk of mixing units."),
 ("Why is the speed of light denoted by c?", "From the Latin celeritas meaning speed; it is exactly 299 792 458 m/s."),
 ("Differentiate a law from a theory.", "A law describes a regular pattern; a theory explains why the pattern occurs, on the basis of evidence."),
 ("Is a scientific theory just a guess?", "No. It is an explanation built on careful testing and critical examination, open to revision with new evidence."),
 ("Why are weather forecasts less reliable for the distant future?", "Tiny differences in temperature, pressure, humidity or wind grow with time and change the outcome."),
 ("How can the claim 'food is harmful during an eclipse' be tested?", "Ask what physical change an eclipse causes; since it is only shadow and no mechanism harms food, the claim has no scientific support."),
 ("Estimate the litres of air breathed per day.", "About 20 000 breaths × 0.5 litre ≈ 10 000 litres.")])],
takeaways=["Science is about **how** we know, not only what we know — observe with a magnifying glass, move with a compass.",
 "**Models** simplify reality by deliberately ignoring unimportant details.",
 "Precise **symbols, SI units and mathematics** form the language of science; mixing units can be dangerous.",
 "A **law** gives a pattern, a **theory** explains it, a **principle** is a broad guiding idea; theories are evidence-based, not guesses.",
 "**Predictions** are reasoned expectations; failed predictions drive improvement.",
 "Use **estimation** to check whether answers are reasonable.",
 "Branches of science are human divisions; real problems need many branches together."])
